.docname {Registry Service}
.include {docs}

.glossary
    | Word | Meaning | Example |
    |---|---|---|
    | Organization | A customer that signed up | `northfield` |
    | System of record | The one place a fact is stored and trusted | `registry` for "which organizations exist" |
    | Status | How far setting up the organization got | `provisioning`, `ready`, `failed`, `suspended` |
    | License status | Whether the organization may use the product | `active`, `suspended`, `expired` |
    | Tier | How the organization is hosted | `pooled` (shared), `dedicated`, `on-prem` |
    | Identity mode | Who signs its people in | `platform` (CrowdVision's Keycloak) or `byo-idp` (its own provider) |
    | HMAC signature | A code from a shared secret and the request body that proves the caller holds the secret | `X-Signature` header |
    | Migration | A SQL file that builds or changes the tables, run at start | `0001_init.up.sql` |

Go service at `backend/registry`, with its own PostgreSQL database. It records which organizations exist and what state each is in. It builds nothing itself: [`provisioner`](provisioner.qd) reads the pending ones and does the work. Domain side: [Identity & Tenancy](../architecture/auth-architecture.qd).

- No route at the edge. Only callers inside the network reach it.
- Makes no outbound call.

---

## Package layout

| Path | Holds |
|---|---|
| `cmd/registry/main.go` | Reads config, runs migrations, opens the pool, mounts routes, shuts down cleanly |
| `internal/api/handler.go` | `Mount` and the four handlers |
| `internal/service/registry.go` | Rules: valid tiers, default identity mode, status changes |
| `internal/store/store.go` | `Organization` and the `Store` interface |
| `internal/store/postgres.go` | `Store` on PostgreSQL with `pgx` |
| `internal/storefake/fake.go` | `Store` in memory, for tests |
| `migrations/0001_init.up.sql` | The `organizations` table |

The signature check is not here: it is `authcontracts.RequireSignature` from [Auth Contracts](../packages/auth-contracts.qd).

---

## What it stores

One table, `organizations`:

| Column | Type | Rule |
|---|---|---|
| `id` | uuid | Made by the database |
| `name` | text | Required, **unique** |
| `display_name` | text | Required |
| `tier` | text | `pooled`, `dedicated` or `on-prem` |
| `host` | text | Optional. Nothing writes it |
| `identity_mode` | text | `platform` (default) or `byo-idp` |
| `issuer` | text | Optional. The organization's own sign-in provider |
| `license_status` | text | `active` (default), `suspended`, `expired` |
| `status` | text | `provisioning` (default), `ready`, `failed`, `suspended` |
| `status_detail` | text | Why it failed |
| `created_at` | timestamptz | Set by the database |

Two state machines live on one row:

.mermaid
    stateDiagram-v2
        direction LR
        state "status" as S {
            [*] --> provisioning: signup
            provisioning --> ready: provisioner, ready
            provisioning --> failed: provisioner, failed
            ready --> suspended: suspend
            provisioning --> suspended: suspend
            failed --> suspended: suspend
        }

- `status` says how far setup got. Only `provisioner` sets `ready` or `failed`.
- `license_status` says whether the customer may use the product. `suspend` sets both to `suspended`.
- Nothing sets `expired`, and nothing moves an organization out of `failed` or `suspended`.

---

## Routes

| Method, path | Who may call | Does | Answers |
|---|---|---|---|
| `GET /health` | anyone | Liveness | `200` |
| `POST /organizations` | anyone who can reach it | Signup | `201`, `400` bad body or tier, `500` |
| `GET /internal/organizations/pending` | signed | Every organization with `status = provisioning` | `200`, `403`, `500` |
| `POST /internal/organizations/{id}/status` | signed | Set `ready`, or `failed` with a detail | `204`, `400`, `403`, `404`, `500` |
| `POST /internal/organizations/{id}/suspend` | signed | Set license and status to `suspended` | `204`, `403`, `404`, `500` |

Signup request, then its `201` answer:

```json
{"name":"northfield","displayName":"Northfield Library","tier":"pooled","identityMode":"platform","issuer":""}
```

```json
{"id":"6f1c…","name":"northfield","displayName":"Northfield Library","tier":"pooled","status":"provisioning","licenseStatus":"active"}
```

`SignupInput` has no JSON tags. Go matches keys without caring about case, so `displayName` fills `DisplayName`.

---

## Signup end to end

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
        autonumber
        participant C as Caller
        participant H as api.signup
        participant S as service.Signup
        participant P as store.Postgres.Create
        participant DB as PostgreSQL
        C->>H: POST /organizations {name, displayName, tier, …}
        H->>H: json decode into SignupInput
        H->>S: Signup(ctx, in)
        S->>S: tier in validTiers?
        S->>S: identityMode empty? use platform
        S->>P: Create(Organization{…, LicenseStatus active, Status provisioning})
        P->>DB: insert into organizations … returning id
        DB-->>P: id
        P-->>S: Organization with ID
        S-->>H: org
        H-->>C: 201 {id, name, displayName, tier, status, licenseStatus}

| Step | Data |
|---|---|
| 1 | `{"name":"northfield","displayName":"Northfield Library","tier":"pooled"}` |
| 2 | `SignupInput{Name:"northfield", DisplayName:"Northfield Library", Tier:"pooled", IdentityMode:"", Issuer:""}` |
| 5 | `IdentityMode` becomes `platform` |
| 7 | `insert … values ('northfield','Northfield Library','pooled',NULL,'platform',NULL,'active','provisioning')`. Empty `host` and `issuer` become `NULL` through `nullif` |
| 11 | `201 {"id":"6f1c…", …, "status":"provisioning","licenseStatus":"active"}` |

### Decisions in signup

.mermaid
    flowchart TD
        B["body"] --> D{"valid JSON?"}
        D -- no --> E1["400 bad body"]
        D -- yes --> T{"tier pooled, dedicated or on-prem?"}
        T -- no --> E2["400 invalid tier"]
        T -- yes --> I["identityMode empty becomes platform"]
        I --> W{"insert works?"}
        W -- no --> E3["500 signup failed"]
        W -- yes --> OK["201"]

**Example.** A second signup named `northfield`: the unique constraint fails the insert, and the caller gets `500 signup failed`, not a "name taken" answer.

**Example.** `identityMode: "sso"`: the service passes it through, the table's check refuses it, the caller gets `500`.

---

## Status update end to end

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
        autonumber
        participant Pv as provisioner
        participant G as RequireSignature
        participant H as api.setStatus
        participant S as service
        participant DB as PostgreSQL
        Pv->>G: POST /internal/organizations/6f1c…/status<br/>X-Signature, {"status":"failed","detail":"…"}
        G->>G: read whole body, put it back
        G->>G: Verify(secret, body, signature)
        G->>H: next handler
        H->>H: decode {status, detail}
        H->>S: MarkFailed(id, detail)
        S->>DB: update organizations set status, status_detail where id
        DB-->>S: 1 row
        H-->>Pv: 204

`setStatus` accepts exactly two values:

.mermaid
    flowchart TD
        A["status in body"] --> R{"ready?"}
        R -- yes --> MR["MarkReady: status ready, detail cleared"]
        R -- no --> F{"failed?"}
        F -- yes --> MF["MarkFailed: status failed, detail kept"]
        F -- no --> E["400 status must be ready or failed"]
        MR --> N{"row found?"}
        MF --> N
        N -- no --> E404["404"]
        N -- yes --> OK["204"]

- Anything else, an empty value included, is `400` with nothing written. A typo never marks a customer live.
- `ready` writes `status_detail = NULL` (`nullif('', '')`), so an old failure reason disappears.

### Suspend

`Suspend` writes two updates, in this order:

1. `license_status = 'suspended'`. Fails: return the error, `status` untouched.
2. `status = 'suspended'`.

The two are not in one transaction. If step 2 fails, the license is suspended and `status` is not.

---

## The signature check

`authcontracts.RequireSignature(secret)` wraps the three `/internal/*` routes:

1. No `X-Signature`: `403 missing signature`.
2. Read the whole body, then put the bytes back so the handler can read them too.
3. Compute `hex(HMAC-SHA256(secret, body))` and compare with `hmac.Equal`, which takes the same time whatever the input.
4. Different: `403 invalid signature`. Same: run the handler.

**Example.** `GET /internal/organizations/pending` has no body. With the fixture secret `an-internal-secret-that-is-at-least-32-bytes`, the right header is `52a0b3efd233aae8d54e811f3e10623f119e3978fd9411c10174b8e5307607b7`.

---

## Start and stop

`main.go`:

1. `config.Load`: a missing `DATABASE_URL` or `INTERNAL_SIGNING_SECRET` stops the process.
2. Run every migration in `file://migrations`. Already up to date is fine; any other error stops the process.
3. Open a `pgxpool`.
4. Mount `/health` and the routes, listen on `ADDR`.
5. On `SIGINT` or `SIGTERM`, stop taking requests and give running ones 10 s.

---

## Configuration

| Variable | Default | Use |
|---|---|---|
| `DATABASE_URL` | required | PostgreSQL connection string |
| `INTERNAL_SIGNING_SECRET` | required | HMAC secret, the same one `provisioner` uses |
| `ADDR` | `:3000` | Listen address |

---

## Tests

```bash
just test registry               # unit, no database
just test registry-integration   # store against a real PostgreSQL, testcontainers
```

| File | Checks |
|---|---|
| `service/registry_test.go` | New organization is `provisioning`; empty identity mode becomes `platform`; unknown tier refused; duplicate name refused by the fake; status changes; suspend sets both, and leaves `status` alone when the license write fails |
| `api/handler_test.go` | `201`; bad tier and bad body `400`; internal routes need a signature; only `ready` and `failed` accepted, a missing status refused; unknown id `404`; store errors `500` |
| `store/postgres_test.go` | Behind `-tags=integration`. Create and get; unique name; pending lists only `provisioning`; not-found on update; license change leaves `status` alone |

---

## Gaps

- **Duplicate name is `500`.** The unique constraint error is not turned into a "name taken" answer.
- **Unknown identity mode is `500`.** Only the database check refuses it.
- **No caller for signup.** Nothing in the repo calls `POST /organizations`, and the edge has no route to it.
- **Suspend is two writes, not one transaction.**
- **`host` and `expired` are never set.**
